# Command ansifilter

## What does this command do:

Strips ANSI/VT100 terminal escape codes from text, or converts them to
HTML/LaTeX/RTF/SVG/BBCode/Pango markup. Used by `myclaude`, `mycodex`, and
`claude-log-view` in `~/tools` to clean colorized session logs into plain,
readable text.

## Installed from

For Fedora install using: sudo dnf install ansifilter
For Raspberry Pi install using: sudo apt install ansifilter
For Mac install using: brew install ansifilter
For Mac container (Fedora Linux, ARM/aarch64) install using: sudo dnf install ansifilter

> `colorized-logs` (providing `ansi2txt`) is an apt/dnf-only fallback when
> `ansifilter` isn't available — it has no Homebrew formula, so `ansifilter`
> is the one option that covers all four hosts.

## Command path

For Fedora run: /usr/bin/ansifilter
For Raspberry Pi run: /usr/bin/ansifilter
For Mac run: /opt/homebrew/bin/ansifilter
For Mac container run: /usr/bin/ansifilter

## Command documentation

Docs can be found at http://andre-simon.de/doku/ansifilter/en/ansifilter.php

## Command options

# Strip ANSI codes from a file, write plain text to stdout
ansifilter -i session.log

# Strip ANSI codes from stdin, write to stdout
cat session.log | ansifilter

# Write filtered output to a file instead of stdout
ansifilter -i session.log -o session.txt

# Follow a growing file like `tail -f`, filtering as it grows
ansifilter -t -i session.log

# Convert to HTML instead of plain text (keeps color as CSS)
ansifilter -H -i session.log -o session.html

# Ignore ANSI formatting entirely, just pass text through
ansifilter -p -i session.log

# Typical cleanup pipeline used by myclaude/claude-log-view:
ansifilter -i session.log | col -b | tr -d '\r' | cat -s
